      "observed": "On the AVR-ISP connector J4, pin 3 (SCK) and pin 4 (MOSI) are both attached to the same global label SCK. The MOSI pin is therefore merged with the SCK net, while the MCU MOSI/PB1 pin (U2.6/U3.6) is only on the separate PWM net.",
      "expected": "A 6-pin AVR ISP interface to the ATtiny should keep SCK and MOSI separate: J4 pin 3 (SCK) should connect to the MCU SCK/PB2 net, and J4 pin 4 (MOSI) should connect to the MCU MOSI/PB1 net (the PWM net in this schematic).",
      "evidence": [
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "Sheet / (fan_controller.kicad_sch), tile 1: J4 AVR-ISP-6 shows pin 1 MISO -> SDA, pin 4 MOSI -> SCK, pin 3 SCK -> SCK, pin 5 RST -> RST."
        },
        {
          "source": "schematic",
          "quote": "Sheet / (fan_controller.kicad_sch), tile 2: U2 and U3 pin 6 is labeled PWM and pin 7 is labeled SCK, indicating MOSI/PB1 and SCK/PB2 are intended as separate nets."
        }
      ],
      "impact": "An ISP programmer connected to J4 would have its MOSI and SCK lines shorted together and would not reach the MCU PB1/MOSI pin. Programming and SPI access would fail, and the MCU PWM/MOSI and SCK nets could be driven together.",
      "confidence": 0.75
    }
  ],
  "summary": "The schematic contains an ISP wiring anomaly: J4 pin 4 (MOSI) is tied to the same global net as J4 pin 3 (SCK), shorting MOSI to SCK instead of connecting it to the separate PWM/MOSI net."
}

